# SOP Workbench in the conversation

Ask questions, author the SOP, and review it in the current desktop session. The CLI saves and verifies records and MCP displays them. By default, the deliverable is the SOP. Starting another model, developing the application, and running a paid audit require separate authorization. Use the tools below when available. If the integration is unavailable, describe the gap and continue the ordinary document workflow using the actual project.

## Show each reply

1. Identify the user-authorized project directory and stable project ID. Reuse the known location. When a new case needs a directory, create a separate one inside the current workspace and preserve the old cases. Global MCP calls require the initialized project's absolute `project_directory`. On `project_not_initialized`, run `formsy sop engine init "<absolute project directory>" --no-project-skills` in the directory the user authorized for setup. Prepare the Workbench or retry with the actual project ID and the same directory. Retain an existing project's identity and history. Initialization only sets up project identity and local resources. The document-only Workbench can operate without a development flow.
2. Write the actual requirement to an input file in that project. Use `workbench prepare` below to prepare potentially relevant atomic conditions, requirement sources, and this round's scope. Use `formsy_workbench_publish_update` with `presentation="overview"` to save the requirement receipt and initial questions. Display the full Workbench and ask the same group of questions. Keep candidate conditions pending an applicability decision.
3. Before **each visible intermediate reply**, call the same tool with `presentation="update"` and record only that reply's changes. After it succeeds, show the corresponding text or question controls. A group of questions counts as one interaction. State when there is no new content. A card remains bound to its original reply, and later replies preserve its contents.
4. When an answer arrives, immediately record it and publish that milestone before writing a long draft. Technical contracts, task handoff, L1 structure, L2 key rules, atomic evidence, and the summary are short deliverable milestones. Save each milestone before starting the next block. Load only the rules needed for each block and produce an inspectable artifact. During longer work, share actual progress in about a minute, showing a completed part first when useful. Use completed work and saved records as progress evidence.
5. For the first summary and delivery after a change, save the final draft bytes for this round. Complete the content self-check and necessary revisions. Update the `selected` preparation and save the current atomic check receipt. Verify that the receipt matches current sources and scope and contains no `not_reviewed` items. Publish the completed self-check milestone and the full Workbench with `presentation="summary"`. Check the successful tool response and confirm that the summary identifies the correct project, document version, receipt digest, and open items. Then use the file-open tool and deliver the main SOP, its status, and unresolved items in the conversation. The final draft can be saved earlier. Wait for the preceding checks before automatically opening the final file, sharing the formal delivery link, or asking for final document confirmation. If the user explicitly asks for an intermediate draft, show it with its self-check status. A completed check can retain genuinely unverified items. Document delivery does not depend on successful application execution.

If publication or reading fails, or if sources or scope change, retain the draft and failure record and report the incomplete delivery. After repair, retry with the original publication ID or retry only the read. Reusing the same decision must not duplicate the record. A standalone document task without Workbench integration can follow the original delivery flow with its limitation stated. A successful tool response establishes that data and display resources were generated. Claim that the user saw the result only with actual host rendering evidence. Avoid fixed waits as a substitute for that evidence.

If only confirmation metadata changes after the user confirms, refresh the source digest, explain the difference, recheck affected items, and rebind the receipt. Use `awaiting_confirmation` until explicit confirmation. Use `user_declared_confirmed` only after explicit confirmation. If the receipt is missing or sources changed, keep self-check or assembly short of `completed` and deliver with the gap stated when appropriate.

To display existing records, call `formsy_workbench_show(project_directory, view="overview"|"summary")`. Overview checks the lightweight version roughly once a second and loads new content only when it changes. A summary stays bound to its original snapshot. Load at most five history rows at a time and expand them on demand. The host controls where cards and text appear. Describe what the current host actually rendered.

## Load requirements and gather evidence

Use the Skill's configured `scripts/sop.py` to invoke the existing CLI as `workbench prepare PROJECT --input PREPARATION.json`. Standalone local document preparation requires no development flow, assistant login, or other model. When connecting global MCP afterward, first establish project identity as described above and prepare records under that identity. Preparation uses the packaged, frozen r12 catalog. Choose condition IDs from the catalog actually read. Use the CLI to expand all atomic obligations for the selected conditions.

First read the actual catalog, triggers, and version with `workbench catalog PROJECT`. Use the repeatable `--condition-id TC04.C02` option to read each selected parent condition and its complete atomic contract. Read the catalog shipped in the installed CLI, independent of a separate TraceAudit Skill installation.

After saving `selected` preparation, run `workbench scaffold PROJECT --round-id UNIQUE_ID --document-source-id REGISTERED_SOURCE_ID --output NEW_CANDIDATE.json` to generate the complete atomic scaffold. Use the configured CLI runtime. The command creates the output exclusively, stores no audit, invokes no model, and gives each atom `review_status=not_reviewed`. In the current session, fill actual comparisons, applicability, quotations, gaps, and next actions before calling `record`. Append preparation first when sources change. `review_status=reviewed` records that the item was reviewed. `evidence_pending` records that comparison is complete and specified material or runtime evidence is still missing. Keep the outcome `unverified` and provide a concrete gap and next action. Preserve the review depth of unclassified historical records.

Keep the original four verdicts and classify gaps separately with `gap_tags`. `uncompared` means the original was not compared, `design_missing` means required design is missing, `decision_pending` means a decision is pending, and `implementation_pending` means implementation verification is pending. Tags can overlap and cannot create a pass or alter a prior failure. For upcoming tasks, record the owning role in `owner`, state if the person is undecided, and include real task IDs in `blocked_tasks`. Identify the source or object, missing evidence, and closure conditions. Unclassified historical receipts stay unclassified.

`design_missing` requires an actual comparison and an applicable design requirement shown to be missing. Record `unsatisfied` and `reviewed` with a valid citation. Show “Unsatisfied” in the top count and condition row, separately from pending items. Pending corresponds to the original `unverified` verdict, and the four headline counts are disjoint. An overlapping gap tag does not replace the original judgment. When evidence is insufficient, keep `unverified` and describe the missing decision or runtime check. Satisfied or inapplicable items have no open gap tags. Use `uncompared` only with `not_reviewed/unverified`. Counts for overlapping tags cannot be summed as an atomic total. Record a new round when evidence arrives and preserve older receipts.

Group the main display by gap tag, with separate counts and filters for unsatisfied and pending items. Show tags in the header and collapsed condition rows. Preserve the original four verdicts in details and history. Keep a known unsatisfied result as unsatisfied. Show untagged history and object checks as unclassified. When rechecking, verify current wording in `limitations`, `change_note`, status summaries, and open questions. Correct stale descriptions in a new receipt and preserve the old bytes.

The document self-check view is read-only and displays items, verdicts, evidence, and unresolved issues. It provides no satisfaction or bulk recheck buttons. When the user needs to make a choice or supply information, use the current Codex session's `request_user_input_async` dialog, retaining the original question ID and actual answer source. If the tool is unavailable, ask in the conversation. Codex continues checking unreviewed items and missing evidence. Preserve all four verdicts, gaps, citation errors, and historical receipts. User input alone grants no document confirmation, stage confirmation, or authorization to begin implementation.

The input `schema_version` is `formsy-review-preparation-0.1`. Fields are `project_id`, unique ASCII `preparation_id`, `scope`, `selection_status` (`proposed` or `selected`), `reason`, `condition_ids`, `sources`, and nonempty `limitations`. Each source provides `id/path/revision/sha256` for an actual project file, with optional `role`. Initially, the requirement input can be the only source. Optional `evidence_candidates` maps atomic IDs to quotation arrays in the same form as the atomic receipt. Preparation verifies source bytes and citation structure. Semantic judgment and satisfaction belong to review. Judgment fields such as `candidate_result` are rejected.

- On receiving a requirement, show its `proposed` scope, sources, and atomic requirements. Leave applicability pending.
- When scope or draft is established, append `selected` preparation with the selection reasons and excluded conditions. Include the saved versions, digests, and candidate passages. Keep candidate evidence unverified.
- During self-check, compare the complete obligation for each item against the original in the current session. Record reasons, quotations, and gaps. Use the existing `workbench record ... --verification-root TRUSTED_ROOT` to save the actual receipt. Then publish the completed self-check and summary. Reuse the byte-pinned `check_refs/aggregate`. Perform the review in the current session and assess each atom on its own evidence.

Start self-check by verifying that the preloaded selection is complete and correct. Compare the actual catalog conditions against requirement triggers. Conditions with the same rationale can be grouped. Add a `selection_review` array to `selected` preparation. Each group contains only `condition_ids`, `decision` (`include/exclude/uncertain`), and `reason`. Every frozen condition must occur exactly once. The union of `include` and `uncertain` must equal the current selection. Uncertainty does not justify exclusion. An exclusion records that the current round did not check the condition and establishes no inapplicability or pass. The CLI verifies coverage and consistency. The current session reviews the substantive reasons and tracks business rules and uncompiled scope separately.

When you find an omitted or incorrectly selected condition, append preparation with additions, removals, retained items, and reasons. Preserve earlier selections. The new receipt automatically binds the current preparation digest. Review again before saving, even if only a selection rationale changes. When correcting the scope of an existing check, set `previous_round_id` and give `scope_change_reason` in the new receipt. Match the latest `selected` preparation. The CLI saves the scope revision, and the old receipt keeps the prior scope. Check every added atom and keep removed items in history without changing their verdicts.

Append preparation, checks, and interactions separately and preserve old records. Preparation establishes neither audit nor recheck. After preparation exists, CLI and MCP reject a `selfcheck/assemble` `completed` event that lacks the current `selected` scope, selection review, or a receipt matching the source digest. A newly classified receipt with `not_reviewed` items also blocks completion. Review each item first. `evidence_pending` can retain genuine gaps. Keep older unclassified receipts compatible at their recorded depth. Document uncompiled PC/KB checks and limitations in Markdown, using no substitute technical atomic IDs.

When sources, scope, conditions, or candidate quotations change, promptly append preparation and show the change in a short milestone. The Workbench observes registered files. Changes put the current receipt up for recheck. The latest overview shows new preparation and atomic requirements without carrying over the old pass count. Historical rounds stay readable. After source changes, an old quotation still represents its original version. Locate it again in the current source before rechecking. The Workbench observes only registered files. Explicitly register new files, changed condition selections, and adoption of a new frozen rule version. Verify a changed frozen catalog with the existing validator and select conditions again.

A final summary is a snapshot. Subsequent confirmations or checks leave the saved summary intact, mark it as historical, and let the user open the latest overview. Refer to a pre-confirmation summary as historical when describing current confirmation status.

If the confirmed text still says it awaits confirmation, preserve its bytes and attach this round's status summary. Include the current version and digest, matching confirmation receipt link, whether earlier confirmation still applies, open items or handoff tasks, and the next action. State the record time and scope. Append a new summary for a new version. This status summary does not authorize development or closure.

Save the status addendum after the confirmation receipt exists. Register its actual `path/revision/sha256` in a later `document_changes` event, publish the closing summary, and verify that the source page can locate it under stage documents. This wrap-up registers the attachment and reuses the original confirmation binding. Preserve the original decision and confirmed text. Only include the addendum as an audit source if it took part in the atomic audit. Save answer records, substantive artifacts, and self-check milestones separately. Deliver saved milestones before starting long blocks.

## Structured events

`formsy_workbench_publish_update` takes explicit `project_directory`, `presentation`, and `event`. Reuse the existing CLI writer, which binds the previous digest automatically. Retry with the same `interaction_id` and identical content to avoid duplicate writes. Give changed content a new ID.

| Event field | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| project_id / project_title | Stable project identity matching the atomic receipt |
| interaction_id / reply_id | Unique ASCII identifiers for each event and visible reply. Use a new ID for a different reply. |
| conversation_id | Actual current Codex session ID. Use the current conversation's own identity. |
| turn_id | Actual host turn ID, when known. Omit when unavailable. |
| phase | scope / product / technical / knowledge / selfcheck / assemble |
| stage_status | in_progress / waiting_user / completed / interrupted |
| document_status | draft / awaiting_confirmation / user_declared_confirmed |
| title / summary | Short action title and a few readable lines of changes. Place detailed evidence in the corresponding fields below. |
| questions | This event's questions and answers. Include id, question, status=pending/answered, and answer. Answered items also have source_locator and source_quote. |
| decisions | This event's decisions. Include id, text, and status=proposed/user_declared/superseded. User declarations require source_locator and source_quote. |
| document_changes | Actual saved project-relative path, revision, sha256 of the real bytes, and summary. Use an empty array when no document changed. |
| pending | Open items added or updated in this event, with id and text. IDs remain stable across rounds and distinct from question and decision IDs. |
| resolves | Explicitly handled item id and reason. Reference a previously registered item. Omitting a prior item does not close it. |
| document_confirmation | Whole or partial document confirmation. Include decision_id and a nonempty sources array with path/revision/sha256 for each registered file version the user explicitly confirmed. |

Omitted `questions/decisions/document_changes/pending/resolves` default to `[]`. The program enforces `execution_performed=false` and `formal_closed=false`. The user confirmation field records a traceable statement and establishes neither identity authentication nor the truth of the content. Keep original question IDs in answers. When adopting or replacing a suggestion, retain the decision ID or record an explicit `superseded` entry. A historical pending answer remains as recorded at the time. The current view joins items by stable ID and removes gaps only with an explicit resolution.

To supersede an earlier answer or suggestion, add `supersedes` with the old question or decision IDs to the new `user_declared` decision. Register the impact of the new decision as a pending item. The current view marks the target as superseded and retains the replacing ID. Preserve the earlier receipt and leave the superseded target superseded. A combined decision supersedes only its corresponding questions, and other applicable scope decisions retain their status. At final confirmation, verify the target versions for all open items. Record proposal or intended-use review separately from pre-implementation patches and supplier checks. Ask confirmation for the current version and keep actual evidence gaps open after document confirmation.

## Change impact, open-item classification, and version confirmation

When a decision already in effect changes content or state, register an impact pending item in the current event. Include `category=document_blocker`, `next_action`, and `impact` with `decision_id`, `modules`, `document_paths`, and `condition_ids`. Use existing stage names for modules. Keep arrays empty for documents and conditions that are not yet located and leave that impact open. Track actual changes to product behavior, technical and data state, tasks, acceptance, and references in the current session. The CLI checks registration and consistency. The current session owns the dependency analysis.

To resolve an impact item, include `reviewed_modules` alongside `id/reason` in `resolves`. List every module under the impact and explain what changed or why it needs no change. Changes to atomic conditions or sources still require updated preparation, verification, and an appended receipt. Marking a module reviewed records the current session's statement, with semantics and actual execution evidenced separately.

Give each new open item a `category` and `next_action`. Use `document_blocker` for items required before finalizing, `conditional_delivery` for items that can remain at conditional delivery, and `before_implementation` for items required before starting implementation. A document blocker prevents a `completed` summary or document confirmation while still allowing clarification, self-check, and draft delivery. The other two categories may remain open at document confirmation and require separate implementation authorization. Show old unclassified items as unclassified. When changing a category, record `classification_reason` and preserve history.

A document confirmation's `decision_id` must refer to a traceable `user_declared` statement in the same event. Each item under `sources` must match its registered version and the current file bytes. Bind only the subset the user confirmed. Record confirmation only with a matching user statement. Show the historical limitation for an older statement with no digest binding. Bind such a statement retroactively only when its original target, then-current version, and frozen digest can be verified. Label it as a retroactive binding and preserve the original statement.

When file bytes or a registered version change, the current view returns to awaiting confirmation and retains the earlier statement in history. Require a matching explicit statement for the new version. Ordinary progress cannot reuse `user_declared_confirmed`. Keep whole-document confirmation, a user statement of satisfaction for an atom, evidence of a pass, and implementation authorization as separate records.

If the save result is uncertain, retry the same `interaction_id` and identical content. The original receipt returns without another write, even if a source changed afterward. It represents the original event and does not restore current confirmation. Use a new ID for changed content. If display reads fail, retry the read independently of decisions and confirmation writes.

The compatibility CLI `workbench publish PROJECT --input EVENT.json` binds the previous record automatically. The legacy `interaction` entry point preserves its existing previous-record validation. Without MCP, write JSON using a file tool and call the configured CLI. Report a failed save. Label a later record as retroactive if it is saved after repair.

## Reply completion and automatic archiving

MCP publishing explicitly binds only the current conversation to the current project. Installed, trusted metadata observation hooks receive `UserPromptSubmit`, `Stop`, and `Interrupt`. They record session, turn, associated reply, and time. They do not use or save prompts or assistant text, access transcript files, call a model, block the session, or resume it. `Stop` updates the card's status to archived. Interruption preserves the record. An old card can change its archive badge while retaining the original reply.

When no actual completion event exists, display the reply record as awaiting that event. A later reply can make the earlier one historical while leaving its `Stop` observation unknown. Automatic archiving changes display only. Confirmation, resolving evidence gaps, and development authorization have their own records. New or changed hooks require host review and trust. Ordinary SOP authoring leaves hook installation and trust unchanged.

Store interactions, archiving, and session binding in `.formsy-sop-interactions/`, `.formsy-sop-lifecycle/`, and `.formsy-sop-sessions/` in the configured workspace, respectively. Keep private data out of Git. The event limit is 128 KiB and the full response limit is 2 MiB. Report an explicit failure above the limit.

## Atomic conditions, rechecks, and user statements of satisfaction

Read the repository's `docs/workbench.md` to pin the trusted verification root and frozen rules. Read the parent condition and all atomic obligations. In the current session, produce the document candidate and use the existing `workbench record` to validate citations and save it. PC/KB without a matching compiled contract remains outside the older technical conditions. An ordinary Markdown self-check has its own evidence and does not imply atomic coverage.

Show document self-check items, conclusions, evidence, and unresolved issues in the read-only Workbench. Keep satisfaction and bulk recheck controls out of this view. When the user needs to choose or supply information, use `request_user_input_async` in the current Codex session and record the original question ID and real answer source. If unavailable, ask in the conversation. Codex continues checking unreviewed items and missing evidence. Preserve the four verdicts, gaps, citation errors, and historical receipts. User input by itself does not confirm a document or stage or authorize starting work.

Return statements and requests to recheck selected gaps to the current session through a host request. If the host declares `experimental["openai/message"]`, use the official `ui/message` extension metadata `target=active` and `send=true` to request direct submission. Keep the current conversation and preserve the user's draft. If unsupported, use normal return and explain that sending may still be required. On rejection or timeout, preserve the statement and avoid automatic resubmission. Delivery is established when the current session actually receives a message with the matching receipt.

A busy session may cause the host to queue a direct submission and send it automatically after the turn. When queued, finish bounded work and let the host handle the queue. Verify each receipt after its actual arrival. A successful submit response or screenshot of the queue only supports an awaiting-receipt state. Leave interruption to the host. When a message arrives, append receipt and verification records and keep the original statement unique. Track other queued messages separately.

After actual receipt, verify the project, receipt, version, and original text. Continue only within the authorized scope. Pause affected operations after a version change. Record a statement as a Workbench user declaration, with model rechecks and actual evidence recorded separately. Append a recheck through the CLI and retain failures. Use a new file name for an ordinary HTML snapshot and identify it as a snapshot. Keep the project title in the returned message and identify synthetic cases separately from actual user document confirmations.

## Optional object dependencies and the PC/KB pilot

When the user asks to trace a particular object, a changed business decision, or a withdrawn source, use the CLI's `workbench graph` and `graph-review` in the current session. Before writing input, read the full parent condition and atomic obligations with `workbench catalog PROJECT --catalog-id pc-kb-pilot-20261016 --condition-id CONDITION`. This pilot compiles PC03/PC05/PC08/PC09 and KB03/KB04/KB05/KB11/KB15. Report other PC/KB checks as document review alongside machine coverage.

Register each object's ID, kind, revision, state, and actual source location. Link source, decision, requirement, design, task, acceptance, and check in sequence. Record the reason and quotation for each edge. List unregistered objects or relationships as uncovered. Submit checks for two objects under the same condition separately. Record an include, exclude, or uncertain decision and rationale for each registered object and catalog condition. Include and uncertain items require all atomic L1/L2 checks. Leave L3 unexecuted. The current session determines relationships and semantics. The CLI enforces version, citations, completeness, and invalidation.

Both commands require `--input` and `--verification-root`. The graph schema is `formsy-dependency-graph-0.1` and the check schema is `formsy-dependency-review-0.1`. Bind each to its previous digest, and bind rechecks to the latest graph. Append versions while preserving old files and object IDs. A withdrawal retains the object and advances its revision. If the input contract is unavailable, read the installed CLI's corresponding guidance before constructing fields.

After a source, decision, or dependency changes, append the graph before rechecking affected objects. Even if changed file bytes have not yet been registered, the Workbench marks previous conclusions for recheck. Reuse an unaffected object's original receipt with its original execution status. A withdrawn upstream source cannot continue supporting a satisfied outcome. Preserve earlier failures and candidate reasoning. The Workbench groups object-level atomic checks under Atomic Conditions and Evidence and groups rechecks and sources on existing pages, without a separate object dependency graph page. Show the objects and versions the user needs, affected contents, and the reason for invalidation. The backend graph protocol, completeness, and completion gate still apply. Use the PC/KB object check entry point for those objects. Viewing records has no effect on message delivery or SOP confirmation.

## Use current supplemental rules

The six document modules carry `sop-rules-addendum-20261016-0.1` for current confirmation points, grouped questions, delivery form, and domain triggers. Read it through the module's common agreement, then record adoption according to the actual project scope. Repository merges and Skill installation do not change an existing project's adoption or upgrade its earlier confirmations or checks.

`CUSTOM:DOM01` through `CUSTOM:DOM08` and Q01–Q24 are document checks outside the atomic counts for frozen TC and the nine-condition PC/KB pilot. Keep domain cards in the current SOP or a named attachment. Register the actual file, revision, and digest through `document_changes` so the card appears in the current interaction and source view. Report results and gaps with their original states. Reserve `condition_ids` for registered TC and PC/KB compiled conditions. Use module or document targets to link open domain items. Referencing a compiled condition does not create a check receipt.

Apply domain filters to the target software being designed. For example, writing a to-do SOP in Codex does not by itself make the to-do product an AI system. The SOP Workbench's message-return capability does not by itself give the target product a notifications requirement. Review a capability when the target software uses it. Keep an unknown capability pending assessment.

Record the optional numeric precision rule `CUSTOM:DOM09` through the same
domain card attachment and source registration. Use its independent source
version, as defined in the workflow module's numeric precision rule
(`.formsy-sop/skills/formsy-development-kit/modules/formsy-sop-workflow/references/numeric-precision-domain.md`).
Keep its document findings separate from actual program receipts in the
requirement acceptance scope.

When a project decision changes, record an impact item and actual document differences. Explicitly append the affected dependency graph and rechecks. An interaction message alone does not identify decision objects in the graph. Preserve each old graph and receipt with its original binding. If a domain condition or Q criterion later needs machine checking, create a new rule version, complete atomic contract, and applicability and version tests. Preserve frozen r12 and `pc-kb-pilot-20261016`.
